claimed: okay-script-lifecycle -- answer and PROVE (not just assert) the lifecycle question okay-script-runtime left open: how does a runtime-compiled scripts long-lived server (okay-jetty serve) get started without blocking the generator, and stopped cleanly later. Hypothesis: no new ScalaScript API needed -- reuse the existing okay idiom already in ChatDemo.main (Resource.run(Jetty.serve(port)(routes)().map { s => ...; Thread.sleep(Long.MaxValue) }).runWith), have the CALLER invoke ScalaScript.run on its own dedicated java.lang.Thread, and stop by calling Thread.interrupt() -- verified in Resource.scala that Resource.run releases all acquired finalizers on ANY Throwable escaping the scope (including InterruptedException from Thread.sleep), so an interrupt cleanly stops the Jetty server via its own release closure, not just abandons the thread. Prove it with a real test: start a real Jetty server through ScalaScript.run on a background thread, confirm it answers HTTP, interrupt, confirm it stops answering and the returned Result carries the InterruptedException.
